  "/dentistry-orthodontics/dental-emergencies/treatments/antibiotics/": {
    description: "Antibiotics can help control a child's dental infection in West Jordan, UT, but the tooth still needs treatment. Learn when they're used. Call (801) 676-3700.",
    eyebrow: "Emergency Treatment",
    lead: "Antibiotics can help control a spreading tooth infection, but they don't fix the tooth itself. Here's how we use them thoughtfully as part of safe emergency care.",
    hero: "/assets/dentistry/emergencies/treatments/antibiotics/hero.webp",
    promises: [
      { icon: "shield", title: "Controls the spread", text: "Antibiotics help slow a spreading infection, especially with swelling." },
      { icon: "tooth", title: "The tooth still needs care", text: "Pulp therapy or extraction is what truly resolves the source." },
      { icon: "heart", title: "Prescribed thoughtfully", text: "Only when they genuinely help, and always with a treatment plan." },
    ],
    sections: [
      {
        heading: "What Antibiotics Can and Can't Do",
        body: [
          "Antibiotics help slow or control the spread of a dental <a href='/dentistry-orthodontics/dental-emergencies/dental-infection/'>infection</a>, especially when there is swelling. They don't, however, cure the source of the problem. The infected tooth still needs treatment, usually <a href='/dentistry-orthodontics/dental-emergencies/treatments/pulp-therapy/'>pulp therapy</a> or an <a href='/dentistry-orthodontics/dental-emergencies/treatments/extractions/'>extraction</a>, to fully resolve it.",
        ],
      },
      {
        heading: "When We Prescribe Them",
        body: [
          "We prescribe antibiotics thoughtfully, typically when an infection is spreading, there is facial swelling, or your child has a fever along with a <a href='/dentistry-orthodontics/dental-emergencies/dental-infection/abscess/'>tooth abscess</a>. We always pair them with a plan to treat the tooth so the infection doesn't return.",
        ],
      },
      {
        heading: "Using Antibiotics Safely",
        body: [
          "If antibiotics are prescribed, a few rules keep them working the way they should.",
        ],
        steps: [
          { title: "Finish the full course", text: "Exactly as directed, even once your child feels better." },
          { title: "Keep the dental appointment", text: "The antibiotics buy time; treating the tooth ends the problem." },
          { title: "Watch the swelling", text: "It should ease, not grow. Worsening swelling means call us." },
          { title: "Know the red flags", text: "Spreading swelling, high fever, or trouble breathing or swallowing needs urgent care." },
        ],
        callout: { tone: "alert", title: "Call right away if:", text: "Swelling worsens or spreads toward the eye or neck, fever climbs, or your child has <strong>any trouble breathing or swallowing</strong>. Call (801) 676-3700 or seek emergency medical care." },
      },
      {
        heading: "Why 'Just Antibiotics' Isn't Enough",
        body: [
          "The infection lives inside or at the root of the tooth, where antibiotics can't fully reach. That's why pain and swelling often return once a course ends if the tooth wasn't treated. Antibiotics are the bridge; treating the tooth is the cure, and doing both is what keeps your child healthy.",
        ],
      },
    ],
    reassurance: "Antibiotics calm the fire. Treating the tooth puts it out. We'll walk you through both, step by step.",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Can antibiotics alone cure my child's tooth infection?", a: "No. Antibiotics can help control the infection and reduce swelling, but the tooth still needs treatment such as pulp therapy or an extraction to truly resolve the problem." },
      { q: "Does every dental infection need antibiotics?", a: "Not always. We prescribe them when an infection is spreading or there's swelling or fever. The most important step is treating the tooth itself." },
      { q: "What if my child feels better halfway through the course?", a: "Finish it anyway, exactly as directed. Stopping early can let the infection rebound and makes antibiotics less effective in the future." },
    ],
    related: [
      { name: "Dental Infection", href: "/dentistry-orthodontics/dental-emergencies/dental-infection/", blurb: "Signs and what to do." },
      { name: "Tooth Abscess", href: "/dentistry-orthodontics/dental-emergencies/dental-infection/abscess/", blurb: "A serious infection." },
      { name: "Emergency Pulp Therapy", href: "/dentistry-orthodontics/dental-emergencies/treatments/pulp-therapy/", blurb: "Treats the source." },
      { name: "Same-Day Appointments", href: "/dentistry-orthodontics/dental-emergencies/same-day-appointments/", blurb: "Urgent care today." },
    ],
  },
